---
import DocsLayout from '@/layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import { Callout, DocsTable } from '@/molecules/DocComponents/DocComponents';

const MODEL_ROWS = [
  ['Builder (FORGE)', 'A strong general coding model; does the bulk of implementation.'],
  ['Reviewer (SENTINEL)', 'A model you trust for rigorous, adversarial reasoning about security and quality — often worth a higher-cost, more careful model.'],
  ['Orchestrator / devops / writer', 'A reliable model; these roles are lighter on raw reasoning.'],
];
---

<DocsLayout breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Docs', href: '/docs' }, { label: 'Guides', href: '/docs/guides' }, { label: 'Enabling a Model' }]}>
  <h1>Enabling a Model</h1>

  <p>
    Different tasks suit different models. OpenFlows lets you choose which model a role uses —
    and switch it live — without a restart. This page walks through choosing and assigning a
    model for a role.
  </p>

  <h2>Where models live</h2>
  <p>
    Models are configured in the platform's AI settings (the Coder dashboard), where you enable
    the models you want available. OpenFlows then <em>references</em> those models for each
    role — so the catalog lives in the platform, and the assignment lives in your team's
    registry.
  </p>

  <h2>Choosing a model for a role</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Make sure the model is enabled</strong> in the platform's AI settings.</li>
    <li><strong>Open the agent registry</strong> for the role you want to change (for example, the builder).</li>
    <li><strong>Set the model</strong> on that role.</li>
    <li><strong>Save.</strong> The change is validated and takes effect on the team's next cycle. No restart, no redeploy.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Which model for which job?</h2>
  <p>
    A sensible starting point:
  </p>
  <DocsTable headers={['Role', 'A sensible model']} rows={MODEL_ROWS} />
  <p>
    You're free to tune from there based on what you observe.
  </p>

  <h2>What you should see</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The role now uses the assigned model for its work.</li>
    <li>The change applied without a restart; the next workspace provisioned for that role reflects the new model.</li>
    <li>The role's model shows in the panel.</li>
  </ul>

  <Callout type="info" title="Availability notes">
    A model that isn't enabled in the platform can't be assigned — enable it first. Changing a
    model affects <em>new</em> sessions for that role; the exact timing depends on when the
    role's next workspace or session is provisioned. This is a per-role control; you can have
    different roles on different models simultaneously.
  </Callout>

  <Callout type="tip" title="Related reading">
    See <a href="/docs/guides/agent-setup">Agent Setup</a> for how model assignment fits
    in the registry. Choosing a more careful model for the reviewer is often worth it, and you
    can tune from there based on what you observe.
  </Callout>
</DocsLayout>
